---
# yamllint disable rule:line-length
# The reference half of 125: a captured REFERENCE went into the shared state List
# unboxed, so reading it back needs no unboxing call — the Φ.hone.cap-fetch marker
# becomes a bare Φ.hone.fetch and nothing else.
#
# The fetch's `type` is the captured object's class, sed-extracted from the marker's
# "L…;" descriptor: 521 turns the fetch into `List.get(counter++)` followed by a
# checkcast to exactly that class, which is what puts the reference back on the
# stack with the type the static lambda$ declares for that parameter.
#
# This is where the positional capture channel pays off (issue #668). The code in
# the marker is the one 114/118 took off the END of `captures` in the same firing
# that peeled the push, so it is the class of THAT capture, whatever else the run
# holds. Reading target.signature's first L-type instead — what the dedicated
# reference peel did before — is only right when the run holds exactly one
# reference, which is why mixed and multi-reference runs were declined outright.
name: cap-fetch-reference
pattern: |
  ⟦
    𝐵-before,
    𝜏-fetch ↦ ⟦ φ ↦ Φ.hone.cap-fetch, code ↦ 𝑒-code ⟧,
    𝐵-after
  ⟧
result: |
  ⟦
    𝐵-before,
    𝜏-fetch ↦ ⟦ φ ↦ Φ.hone.fetch, type ↦ 𝑒-type ⟧,
    𝐵-after
  ⟧
when:
  matches: ['^L[^;]*;$', 𝑒-code]
where:
  - meta: 𝑒-type
    function: sed
    args:
      - 𝑒-code
      - '"s/^L//"'
      - '"s/;$//"'
